## Week 2 — Mesh, Convergence, and the Lies FEA Tells {#sec:week2-12}

### Why the Mesh Decides Everything {#sec:mesh-12}

Last week you accepted the default mesh. This week you learn why that mesh might have been hiding a 30% error — and how to know when you can trust a result.

Recall the FEA chain: the solver finds *displacement* very accurately even on a coarse mesh, but *stress* is computed by differentiating displacement, and differentiation amplifies any roughness in the solution. Where stress changes rapidly over a short distance — at holes, fillets, and corners — a coarse mesh simply cannot capture the peak. It averages it away.


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![A mesh convergence study. As the number of elements increases, peak stress climbs and then flattens to a stable value. The coarse default mesh under-predicts the true peak by a large margin.](05-01-FEA-SolidWorks_files/figure-pdf/fig-convergence-12-1.pdf){#fig-convergence-12 fig-align='center'}
:::
:::


Figure \@ref(fig:fig-convergence-12) is the single most important picture in this chapter. A **convergence study** means running the same study several times with progressively finer meshes and watching the peak stress. When refining the mesh further stops changing the answer meaningfully — say, less than a 5% change between two refinements — the result has **converged** and you can trust it. We measure that change with a simple percentage difference:

\begin{equation}
\%\,\text{change} = \left| \frac{\sigma_{fine} - \sigma_{coarse}}{\sigma_{fine}} \right| \times 100\%
(\#eq:convergence-12)
\end{equation}

::: {.callout-concept}
**Key Concept: Convergence**  
A result is trustworthy not because it looks pretty but because **refining the mesh no longer changes it.** A single run on a default mesh is a guess. A converged study is an answer. Use Equation \@ref(eq:convergence-12) to put a number on it.
:::

### Stress Concentrations and the Singularity Trap {#sec:singularity-12}

Why does stress pile up at that inside corner of your L-bracket? Because geometry that forces the load to "turn a sharp corner" concentrates the flow of stress, exactly like water speeding up where a river narrows. We quantify it with the **stress concentration factor** $K_t$:

\begin{equation}
\sigma_{max} = K_t \, \sigma_{nom}
(\#eq:kt-12)
\end{equation}

where $\sigma_{nom}$ is the nominal (average) stress you would calculate ignoring the feature, and $K_t$ depends only on the geometry. A generous fillet radius gives a small $K_t$; a tight radius gives a large one. Figure \@ref(fig:fig-kt-12) shows the classic relationship for a stepped shaft in bending.


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Stress concentration factor versus fillet radius. Larger radii dramatically reduce the peak stress, which is why adding a fillet is the cheapest design fix in engineering.](05-01-FEA-SolidWorks_files/figure-pdf/fig-kt-12-1.pdf){#fig-kt-12 fig-align='center'}
:::
:::


Now the dangerous part. A real fillet has a finite radius, so its peak stress is finite and **will converge** as you refine the mesh. But a *perfectly sharp* re-entrant corner in CAD has zero radius — mathematically, the stress there is infinite. This is a **stress singularity.** No matter how fine you make the mesh, the stress at a sharp corner keeps climbing and never settles. Figure \@ref(fig:fig-singularity-12) shows the two behaviours side by side.


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![The convergence test that separates real results from numerical garbage. A filleted feature converges to a finite stress; a sharp re-entrant corner is a singularity whose stress climbs forever and must never be reported.](05-01-FEA-SolidWorks_files/figure-pdf/fig-singularity-12-1.pdf){#fig-singularity-12 fig-align='center'}
:::
:::


::: {.callout-warning}
**Common Mistake:** A student refines the mesh, sees the stress at a sharp corner jump from 300 to 600 to 1200 MPa, and panics that the part is failing. The part is fine — the *model* has a sharp corner that does not exist on the real manufactured part (which always has at least a tiny radius from the tooling). The fix is to **add the real fillet to the CAD geometry**, not to trust the runaway number.
:::

### Boundary Conditions: Garbage In, Garbage Out {#sec:bc-12}

The mesh is only half the story. The other half is whether your fixtures and loads match reality. A "Fixed Geometry" fixture means *perfectly rigid, zero movement* — but a real bolted joint flexes a little. Over-constraining a model makes it look artificially stiff and can hide real stress. Likewise, dumping a force onto a single point creates an artificial singularity, just like a sharp corner.

::: {.callout-discussion}
**Group Discussion:** Your L-bracket lab model was "Fixed" on its back face. In reality it is held by two bolts. List three ways the real bolted joint behaves differently from a perfectly fixed face. Which of those differences would make the *real* part more stressed than your model predicts?
:::

::: {.callout-activity}
**Lab Connection — Week 2 Lab:** This week you will take a flat steel plate with a central hole (`plate_with_hole.SLDPRT`) loaded in tension. First you will calculate the expected peak stress *by hand* using the nominal stress and a published $K_t$ for a hole in a plate (Equation \@ref(eq:kt-12)). Then you will run the FEA **four times** with progressively finer meshes, record the peak von Mises stress each time, and apply Equation \@ref(eq:convergence-12) to find where it converges. Finally you will compare your converged FEA peak to your hand calculation. They should agree within roughly 10% — and if they do, you have just *validated* your own simulation skills.
:::

### Week 2 Lab Guide: Convergence Study and Hand-Calc Check {#sec:lab2-12}

**Hand calculation first (do this before touching the computer).** For a wide plate of width $w$, thickness $t$, with a central hole of diameter $d$, under tension force $P$:

- Nominal stress on the reduced section: $\sigma_{nom} = \dfrac{P}{(w - d)\,t}$
- A hole in a plate has a well-known concentration factor of about $K_t \approx 3$ for a small hole.
- Predicted peak: $\sigma_{max} = K_t\,\sigma_{nom}$ from Equation \@ref(eq:kt-12).

Record your predicted peak stress.

**Then the convergence study.** Set up the static study (material: AISI 1020, one end fixed, tension load on the other end). Run it four times, each time reducing the global element size (or using local mesh control around the hole):


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Worked example of the Week 2 lab convergence table. Each refinement is compared with the previous run until the percentage change drops below 5%.](05-01-FEA-SolidWorks_files/figure-pdf/fig-lab2-results-12-1.pdf){#fig-lab2-results-12 fig-align='center'}
:::
:::


In the worked example of Figure \@ref(fig:fig-lab2-results-12), the jump from Fine to Very-fine is $|169-166|/169 \times 100 = 1.8\%$ — below our 5% threshold, so the result has converged at about 169 MPa, agreeing nicely with the 170 MPa hand calculation. **Your numbers will differ; what matters is that you can demonstrate convergence and a sensible match.**

::: {.callout-recall}
**Theory Recall:** The reason your coarse mesh read low (142 MPa) is exactly the under-prediction shown in Figure \@ref(fig:fig-convergence-12) — a coarse mesh cannot resolve the steep stress gradient around the hole. The reason your hand calc and converged FEA agree is that both are describing the same physical stress concentration captured by $K_t$ in Equation \@ref(eq:kt-12).
:::

::: {.callout-check}
**Quick Check:**  
1. If two successive mesh refinements give 210 MPa and 214 MPa, what is the percentage change, and has the study converged?  
2. Your FEA peak keeps doubling every time you refine the mesh near a corner. What does this tell you, and what should you do?
:::

